Ventricular Ectopics
Cardiovascular
Sensation of skipped beats, Premature beat with compensatory pause, Broad unassociated QRS on ECG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-048
Reason for Visit: Acute anxiety related to a sensation of the heart stopping and "thudding" heavily in the chest while trying to sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient complaining of a sudden, strong thud in the chest followed by a pause, causing severe anxiety that their heart was failing. While suggestive of benign ectopic beats, urgent after-hours assessment was required to clinically exclude a sustained tachyarrhythmia, acute ischemia, or heart failure, and to provide immediate reassurance to prevent an inappropriate emergency department presentation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My heart keeps skipping a beat and then doing a massive, heavy thud that makes me catch my breath, and I'm terrified to fall asleep."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced this evening when the patient lay down in bed. Describes an intermittent subjective sensation of the heart skipping a beat, followed by a sudden strong thud in the chest. Increased awareness of the heartbeat when lying quietly. Reports brief episodic lightheadedness accompanying the heavy thud, but denies true syncope, severe exertional chest pain, or resting dyspnoea. The patient has been working long hours, is highly fatigued, and reports heavy coffee consumption today. KEY SYMPTOMS: Intermittent subjective sensation of the heart skipping a beat. Palpitations often described as a sudden strong thud in the chest. Increased awareness of heartbeat when lying quietly in bed at night. Brief episodic lightheadedness. Exacerbation by caffeine, stress, or fatigue.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly anxious and unable to sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, compounding the anxiety surrounding the cardiac symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 70 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Anxious, frequently placing a hand over the chest, but in no acute physical distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Normal baseline heart rate and blood pressure. Pulse palpation reveals a regular underlying rhythm interrupted intermittently by a premature beat followed by a distinct compensatory pause. Auscultation confirms premature, loud heart sounds followed by a pause and a forceful post-ectopic beat (the "thud"). No murmurs, no S3/S4 gallops, no elevated jugular venous pressure. Lungs are clear. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Sensation of skipped beats, Premature beat with compensatory pause (confirmed on palpation/auscultation), Broad unassociated QRS on ECG (anticipated on formal tracing).
Environmental Observations: Empty coffee mugs visible in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ventricular Ectopics (Premature Ventricular Contractions - PVCs) - Status: Stable/Benign
Differentials:

Atrial Fibrillation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The underlying rhythm is regular, only interrupted by occasional pauses, rather than a completely chaotic, irregularly irregular rhythm without a baseline pattern.

Ischemic Heart Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of exertional or resting chest pain, dyspnea, or diaphoresis; symptoms are purely a mechanical sensation of the beat itself.

Heart Block (e.g., Mobitz II) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a palpated/auscultated premature beat causing the pause, rather than a completely dropped beat without a preceding premature contraction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient describes the classic sensation of premature ventricular contractions (PVCs)—a "skipped" beat followed by a forceful compensatory "thud"—exacerbated by fatigue and high caffeine intake. Clinical examination definitively confirms an underlying regular rhythm interrupted by premature beats with compensatory pauses, with a structurally normal heart and stable hemodynamics. Urgent assessment was necessary purely to alleviate severe acute health anxiety by diagnosing this benign phenomenon clinically, thereby preventing an unnecessary panic-driven emergency hospital admission overnight and allowing the patient to sleep safely.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided extensive reassurance regarding the completely benign nature of the ectopic beats, emphasizing that the heart is not stopping or failing. Arranged a routine outpatient 12-lead ECG and basic blood work (electrolytes, thyroid) to formally document the ectopy and rule out hypokalemia.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to practice the "4-7-8" breathing technique (inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 7, exhale for 8) when lying in bed. By intentionally slowing the breathing, the patient drastically increases vagal tone, which suppresses the ectopic pacemaker cells in the ventricle. The reward is a rapid, noticeable decrease in the thudding sensation, allowing for restful sleep.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Routine General Practice review within a week for an ECG and to discuss a Holter monitor if symptoms persist despite lifestyle changes.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops central, crushing chest pain, extreme shortness of breath, a sustained racing heart rate (>120 bpm for more than 15 minutes), or experiences a fainting spell, they must seek emergency medical care.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on clinical auscultation; formal ECG is required to definitively classify the ectopic beat; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Ectopic beats are extremely common and benign in structurally normal hearts. They typically resolve when physiological stressors like caffeine, stress, and sleep deprivation are removed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Cease all caffeine. Practice slow breathing. Seek emergency care only if you faint, develop severe chest pain, or the heart races continuously.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Due to stress and caffeine, a cell in the bottom chamber of your heart is occasionally firing early. Your heart then pauses for a fraction of a second to reset, and the next beat is much stronger to clear the extra blood—this is the heavy "thud" you feel. It is terrifying, but it is a harmless electrical hiccup, not a sign of heart failure.
Actionable Steps: Throw out any remaining coffee. Lie on your right side tonight (which can sometimes make the beats less noticeable). Practice your slow breathing.

